-- =========================================================================
-- Prover routers — the 8→2 consolidation's two strategy entry points,
-- Fenel-bodied. expr-substrate Phase 6b (brief:
-- `workstreams/expr-substrate/briefs/phase-6b-prover-consolidation.md`).
--
-- The locked decision (roadmap § Proving + solving): `ProofStrategy =
-- Structural | Smt`. The 8 per-proposition-shape `Prover` instances collapse
-- to two — `Prover Structural` (propose = `prover:structuralPropose`) and
-- `Prover Smt` (propose = `prover:smtPropose`). These two routers are the
-- propose methods those instances name.
--
-- `structuralPropose` is the "ONE prover dispatched internally by proposition
-- shape": it pattern-matches the proposition's $tag (tag-only `case` arms —
-- the value's named fields are ignored) and delegates to the existing
-- untrusted proposers BY NAME. It calls them via their codeNames
-- (`decidableSetProverPropose` etc.); the seam adds fnId-keyed native aliases
-- so the codeName→spec.id→native lookup resolves (the codeName≠fnId-suffix
-- reconciliation otherwise leaves the native unreachable from a Fenel call —
-- see modules/prover.ts). The two per-case CHECKS (compilableSemanticCheck /
-- partialEvaluatorSoundnessCheck) are NOT routed here — they are
-- `(testCase) -> MaybeBool` per-case checks, not `proposition -> Result`
-- proposers; their law tests call the natives directly (6c owns lifting them
-- to honest Proposition shapes). Anything else (incl. those two
-- non-`Prop`-prefixed shapes) ⇒ the canonical `Err OutOfScope`.
--
-- decide-substrate Phase 3 (the theory-family completion tail) — WHY THE CLOSED
-- `case` STAYS (the dispatch-key blocker, documented per the brief's fallback;
-- the Id-resolve GAP-#1 precedent). The design (`design/deciding.md` §4) named
-- "structuralPropose's closed case opens into dispatch," analogous to the
-- witness/enumerate cascades that opened into per-shape DecidableMembership
-- faces (Phase 3 Step A). But claim-shape dispatch does NOT fit the same
-- mechanism, for two reasons:
--   1. THE CLAIM SHAPES ARE A MIX OF ENTITIES AND MARKERS. Only `Judgment`
--      (JEmpty / JInhabited, judgment.fenel) and `PropMonotonic` (a registered
--      1-case entity) are dispatch-keyable registered types. The eq / forall /
--      subset / disjoint / empty / inhabited PRESENTATIONS (PropEq / PropForall /
--      PropSubset / …) survive ONLY as `$tag` MARKERS at the TS `$`-key boundary
--      (prover/types.fenel; the closed Proposition sum RETIRED at Phase-2 K4) —
--      they are NOT registered entities, so the typeclass registry has nothing to
--      key a per-shape instance on. Nominal `ByType` dispatch (the live mechanism,
--      Step A) can route JEmpty / JInhabited / PropMonotonic but NOT the markers.
--   2. IT WOULD REVERSE THE 6b CONSOLIDATION. The 8 per-proposition-shape Prover
--      instances (Prover PropSubset, Prover PropEq, …) DELIBERATELY collapsed to
--      two (Prover Structural + Prover Smt) at expr-substrate Phase 6b, replacing
--      per-shape instances with this ONE internally-shape-dispatched router.
--      Re-splitting them re-grows exactly what 6b removed.
-- Registering each presentation marker as a dispatch-keyable entity (to unblock
-- (1)) would re-grow the closed-sum the K4 retirement removed — a regression, not
-- a subsumption. So this `case` IS the correct realization of "ONE prover
-- dispatched internally by claim shape"; it stays. (A genuine Fenel-side
-- resolve-instance-then-invoke-its-proposer would ALSO need a dynamic-fnId invoke,
-- which is audit-gated `bare-string-callable` — the same wall.)
--
-- `smtPropose` is an honest stub: the SMT bridge (Z3 / cvc5) is a
-- post-workstream arc, so it returns `Err OutOfScope` until a real second
-- route exists.
--
-- Both return `Result UnprovedReason ProofTerm` directly (the proposer
-- natives already return `{$tag: Ok/Err, _0}`; the routers pass that through).
-- The Err arms construct via the registered `Err` / `OutOfScope`
-- constructors (the `construct` op authors the `$tag`).
-- =========================================================================

module prover where

@label "Structural prover (propose router)"
@description "The `Prover Structural` propose method (expr-substrate Phase 6b 8→2 consolidation; JUDGMENT-NATIVE arms decide-substrate Phase 2 K3). ONE prover dispatched internally by claim shape: pattern-matches the claim $tag and delegates to the matching untrusted proposer — JEmpty/JInhabited (the one lattice judgment, the K2+ producer currency) ⇒ decidableSetProver (whose plan recognizer shape-recognizes the composed set); PropEq ⇒ reductionProver, PropSubset/PropDisjoint/PropEmpty/PropInhabited (compat until K4) ⇒ decidableSetProver, PropForall ⇒ sampleTestProver, PropMonotonic ⇒ monotonicityProver. Any other shape (incl. the non-Prop-prefixed Compilable/PartialEvaluator per-case CHECKS, which are not proposition proposers) ⇒ Err OutOfScope. The kernel re-checks every term the delegate produces."
@tags "prover" "structural" "propose" "router" "judgment" "expr-substrate" "phase-6b" "decide-substrate" "phase-2"
structuralPropose : (proposition: Any) -> Result UnprovedReason Any
structuralPropose proposition =
  case proposition of
    JEmpty -> decidableSetProverPropose proposition
    JInhabited -> decidableSetProverPropose proposition
    PropEq -> reductionProverPropose proposition
    PropSubset -> decidableSetProverPropose proposition
    PropDisjoint -> decidableSetProverPropose proposition
    PropEmpty -> decidableSetProverPropose proposition
    PropInhabited -> decidableSetProverPropose proposition
    PropForall -> sampleTestProverPropose proposition
    PropMonotonic -> monotonicityProverPropose proposition
    _ -> Err (OutOfScope { note = "Structural prover: no rule for this proposition shape" })

@label "SMT prover (propose router)"
@description "The `Prover Smt` propose method (expr-substrate Phase 6b 8→2 consolidation). The external-solver (Z3 / cvc5) bridge slot — NOT integrated in this workstream; an honest stub returning Err OutOfScope. When the SMT arc lands, a certificate re-check via the Structural prover (where possible) goes here; the kernel stays the single trust gate."
@tags "prover" "smt" "propose" "router" "stub" "expr-substrate" "phase-6b"
smtPropose : (proposition: Any) -> Result UnprovedReason Any
smtPropose proposition =
  Err (OutOfScope { note = "Smt bridge not integrated (post-workstream arc)" })
